%erg 0.1
Title: The phase enumeration can match a literal inside a comment
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-16T06:00Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---

## Context

Asked for by review round 2 of PR 592 and never filed; `/verify-gate` round 1
caught the omission. Filed here so the finding is not lost with the branch.

`tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` does not read the phase set from a
declaration — none exists — so it discovers it by regular expression over the
source of `scheduler.js` and `bootstrap.js`, and guards the result with a
`phases.size >= 12` floor.

A regex over source cannot tell code from prose. A phase name written inside a
comment is matched exactly as a phase name written in an assignment, so the
enumeration can count a string that no code path can ever produce, and the
floor is satisfied by the wrong thing.

## Why it is worth a ticket rather than a shrug

The failure is silent and it is in the *auditing* layer, which is the layer
whose whole job is to be noisy. A test that over-counts passes; the floor was
put there precisely to catch an under-count, so nothing is watching the other
direction.

It also compounds: PR 592 had to repair this regex once already, because
0795's computed phase key silently dropped out of a pattern that expected
quoted keys. Two defects in one enumeration in one branch is a shape, not an
accident.

## Relationship to ticket 0801

0801 replaces the enumeration with a declared alphabet, which removes the class
entirely. **This ticket is not a substitute for it and must not be used as
one.** It is the narrow repair for as long as the scrape is what the suite
relies on — and 0801 itself names this regex as something that must be
re-verified with a positive control before it is trusted, because a `setPhase`
funnel silently empties two of its three extractors.

If 0801 lands first, close this as superseded rather than implementing it.

## Relevant files

- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` — the three extractors and the
  `phases.size >= 12` floor.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js`, `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — the
  scraped sources; every phase name in a comment in either is a false match
  waiting to happen.

## Test

Red first, and the control is the whole point: add a comment containing a
plausible phase-name literal that no code produces, and assert the enumeration
does **not** count it. Against today's extractors that assertion fails, which
is the red step. Then assert the floor still fires when a real phase is
removed, so the repair has not simply made the test blind in the other
direction.

## Verification

- [ ] The comment-literal assertion fails before the fix, with the output recorded
- [ ] The floor still fails when a genuine phase is deleted — run it, do not assume
- [ ] `make check` green

## Invariants

- The enumeration keeps failing when a phase is added without a label, which is
  what it exists for.

## Exit criteria

A phase name appearing only in a comment is not counted, and both directions of
the enumeration — over-count and under-count — have an assertion that has been
seen to fail.
